\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"9e6a7463f88eb72894a2760eaeb44d8b301ca041d32a1ba70c4e17c53f2824cf"},"seconds":32.357786893844604,"seed":507544976179589968,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nThe gcd is 1, which confirms that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists. Now, we backtrack using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\nFrom the second step, we have:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\]\n\nFrom the first step, we have:\n\\[ 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nSubstituting the expression for 159 into the equation for 1, we get:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis equation shows that:\n\\[ 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is \\( n = 9 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{9}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nThe gcd is 1, which confirms that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists. Now, we backtrack using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\nFrom the second step, we have:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\]\n\nFrom the first step, we have:\n\\[ 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nSubstituting the expression for 159 into the equation for 1, we get:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis equation shows that:\n\\[ 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is \\( n = 9 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]","id":"number_theory:231"}
